We got a question about trends in motor vehicle injuries in New Haven and Waterbury compared to other cities and Connecticut, if anyone would like to help investigate (@Thomas Murphy said he might have extra time). The website https://www.ctcrash.uconn.edu/ has all the crash data, and we usually look at total number of crashes, number of fatal crashes, and number of crashes with a fatality or injury.   Then break it down between two groups: drivers/passengers and pedestrians/cyclists/other pedestrians.  (as a benchmark, I think about 85% of the crashes with drivers would have no injury, but if you look just at the pedestrian/cyclist group then only about 15% of the crashes would have no injury).

Could we look at the trends in these six indicators over time, maybe from 2015 to end of 2025?  Since the numbers can get much smaller for the pedestrian group we might need to use some different methods, like pooling longer periods of time, to see if there are any significant changes.  For now, just a table of the numbers in each group by year would be helpful though.

My hypothesis would be that there were a lot of crashes prior to COVID, then a drop for a few months during COVID, but then a record-high rate of crashes around 2020-2022, and then a significantly lower level of crashes since then (with recent years having the lowest levels). However, I'm not sure if that would apply to all groups (for example, Hartford or Waterbury might have a very different trend from New Haven) and which differences would be considered to be statistically meaningful.  One additional factor to consider is that New Haven and Stamford's downtown populations have grown considerably since 2015.